essay:
One point six four. That’s the margin that separated the preseason favorite from perfection and the perennial fifth-place finisher from vindication. Rasheeing the Scene nudged past General Ken-obi 155.66 to 154.02 in a photo finish that the group chat will be litigating until Thanksgiving — Josh Allen’s 25.86 and Bucky Irving’s 23.0 barely outpacing Drake Maye’s 23.22 and Drake London’s 22.0 in a game where every decimal mattered. The franchise everyone measures themselves against just measured up short, and the all-time series between these two is now knotted at three apiece.

{{media:week4-essay-opener}}

Which means the only undefeated team left in the Jailyard is — checks notes — the Legion of Bouz. Preseason rank: ninth. Current rank: first. Record: 4-0. Patrick Mahomes dropped 27.3, Christian McCaffrey added 23.1, and Amon-Ra St. Brown contributed 22.5 in a methodical 13.12-point dismantling of MHJTIME that felt less like an upset and more like a confirmation of something nobody wanted to admit back in August: the inaugural champion never forgot how to win.

Meanwhile, our Lock of the Week — Kittler on the Roof minus five over Ghastly Grayskull Gang — didn’t just lose. It got vaporized. Ghastly dropped 176.42 points, the highest total of the week, and blew the defending champion out by 25.96. Omarion Hampton’s 25.0, DK Metcalf’s 21.1, and Quentin Johnston’s 19.8 turned the team with the “thin receiver room” into an offensive juggernaut that outscored every other team in the league. Kittler had Puka Nacua pouring in 29.5 — the week’s best WR performance — and it didn’t matter. The Lock crashed, the defending champion took its first L, and the group chat’s confidence in this column’s gambling advice is at an all-time low.

{{media:week4-lock-bust}}

The cruelest stat of Week 4 doesn’t belong to the losers at the top. It belongs to the losers at the bottom. Ashton Jeanty — the top individual performer in the entire league — went off for 32.5 points. He did it for Chudders Football Team. Chudders lost by 21.36. The rebuild has the best player performance of the week and a 0-4 record. That’s not a rebuilding year; that’s the universe testing your resolve while whispering “but look at what’s coming.”

{{media:week4-jeanty-irony}}

Three teams sit at 0-4 — Chudders, Father Time, and Burden of Etienne-y Woody — and the paths couldn’t diverge more sharply. Chudders has the plan and the capital. Father Time scored 94.62, the week’s low, and got demolished by Noble FFT by 48.76 in the season’s biggest blowout. Burden had George Pickens erupt for 29.4 and Justin Jefferson add 17.6 and still lost to the Sleeping Giants by 31.6, because when your supporting cast is a kicker and a linebacker rounding out the top five, the star power is theatrical, not functional.

At the quarter pole — four weeks through a fourteen-week regular season — one truth is crystallizing: this league’s hierarchy looks nothing like the preseason blueprint. The consensus top three (Ken-obi, Noble, MHJTIME) are a combined 6-6. The preseason bottom three (Father Time, Sleeping Giants, Boonist Monks) are 6-6 too. And the only perfect team was ranked ninth. Somewhere between the projections and the scoreboard, the Jailyard became a league where the script gets rewritten weekly and the group chat’s hot takes expire faster than milk in August.

Four weeks. One unbeaten. Three winless. And the question nobody in the group chat wants to ask: what if the preseason rankings were just fiction we told ourselves to survive the offseason?
